nss_ipv4/nss_ipv6: bound the conn_sync_many count by the buffer it arrived in

The sync-many response carries an array of per-connection statistics and a
count of how many it holds, and nothing on the way to ECM's

	for (i = 0; i < nicsm->count; i++)

in ecm_nss_ipv4_connection_sync_many_callback() tests that count against
anything. With count = 0xffff and ~100-byte records that walks megabytes past
the response buffer, feeding each record into a connection-hash lookup and a
conntrack timeout update from NAPI context. Clamp it in the message handler,
which both that callback and the driver's own loop route through.

The quantity to clamp against is size. index and size are request fields - the
host fills them in before sending and the firmware returns them untouched,
writing only next and count - so size describes the buffer the requester
allocated and handed to nss_ipv4_tx_with_size(), which is the buffer the array
arrived in, and which the firmware sizes its batch from. Cap it at PAGE_SIZE
anyway, so a firmware that did overwrite the field cannot widen the bound past
the largest buffer this path is ever handed.

ncm->len is not a candidate. The firmware leaves it at the request length, so it
computes a capacity of zero and every count clamps to zero: on this board that
discarded the statistics of every accelerated connection, leaving conntrack byte
counts and the counters of every virtual interface reading near zero while a
gigabit flowed through them. Silently, because nss_warning is compiled out
unless the debug level is raised. Nor can ncm->len be made to carry the array
length - the length check above this switch rejects any message longer than
struct nss_ipv4_msg.

The test this replaces lived in the stats helper and compared count against
size, which was the right quantity, but it guarded only the driver's own loop
and it returned rather than clamping, so it could not have protected ECM's.

Both families change together; the IPv4 and IPv6 paths are line-for-line
identical here.

Based on a patch by Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>, which chose
ncm->len for the bound.

--- a/nss_ipv4.c
+++ b/nss_ipv4.c
@@ -120,7 +120,42 @@
 		nss_ipv4_stats_conn_sync(nss_ctx, &nim->msg.conn_stats);
 		break;
 
-	case NSS_IPV4_TX_CONN_STATS_SYNC_MANY_MSG:
+	case NSS_IPV4_TX_CONN_STATS_SYNC_MANY_MSG: {
+		/*
+		 * index and size are request fields: the host fills them in before
+		 * sending and the firmware returns them untouched, writing only
+		 * next and count. So size, not ncm->len, is what describes the
+		 * buffer this array arrived in - the requester allocates it and
+		 * hands it to nss_ipv4_tx_with_size(), and the firmware sizes its
+		 * batch from the same field.
+		 *
+		 * ncm->len cannot serve: the firmware leaves it at the request
+		 * length, so it computes a capacity of zero, every count clamps to
+		 * zero, and the statistics of every accelerated connection are
+		 * discarded - silently, because nss_warning is compiled out unless
+		 * the debug level is raised. Nor can it be made to serve: the
+		 * length check above would reject the message outright if it ever
+		 * grew to cover the array.
+		 *
+		 * Cap size at PAGE_SIZE regardless: nss_core_send_cmd() refuses
+		 * to send a buffer larger than that, so the cap is the allocation
+		 * itself, and a firmware that overwrote size cannot widen it.
+		 *
+		 * Clamp rather than return, and do it here rather than in the stats
+		 * helper, because the registered callback below iterates the same
+		 * count with no test of its own.
+		 */
+		struct nss_ipv4_conn_sync_many_msg *nicsm = &nim->msg.conn_stats_many;
+		uint32_t size = (nicsm->size > PAGE_SIZE) ? PAGE_SIZE : nicsm->size;
+		uint32_t max = (size < sizeof(*nicsm)) ? 0 :
+			(size - sizeof(*nicsm)) / sizeof(struct nss_ipv4_conn_sync);
+
+		if (nicsm->count > max) {
+			nss_warning("%px: conn_sync_many count %u exceeds message capacity %u",
+					nss_ctx, nicsm->count, max);
+			nicsm->count = max;
+		}
+
 		/*
 		 * Update driver statistics on connection sync many.
 		 */
@@ -128,6 +163,7 @@
 		ncm->cb = (nss_ptr_t)nss_ipv4_conn_sync_many_msg_cb;
 		break;
 	}
+	}
 
 	/*
 	 * Update the callback and app_data for NOTIFY messages, IPv4 sends all notify messages
